A module that replaces a replaceable module may no longer use the module it replaces other than by replacing it: it may not import that module, or depend on a module that depends on it. In the compiled program every use of the replaceable module is a use of the replacement, so such a replacement and the client depended on each other, although the module graph that Dafny checked was acyclic. A call cycle through the two modules got no termination check, and a lemma in it proved `false`, with every lemma given a body and nothing marked as an assumption; the same cycle through functions overflowed the stack at run time, and through datatypes gave types without values. Such a replacement is now a resolution error that shows the dependency path, for example `FooImpl -> Bar -> Foo`. The hidden option `--legacy-replacement-cycles` restores the old rule, for comparison and migration only (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/405).
